Title:
WRAITH — browser hook framework

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
WRAITH is a modern browser-hooking and blind-XSS callback framework intended as a clean-room successor to BeEF plus XSS-Hunter/ezXSS-style tooling. It provides a small `hook.js` payload that establishes a persistent WebSocket channel back to an operator console for interactive in-browser control, alongside “blind-XSS loot” collection (origin/URL/referrer, non-HttpOnly cookies, DOM dump, screenshot). A key differentiator is “Page Mirror,” which lets the operator navigate the target application through the victim’s same-origin session (useful when cookies are HttpOnly and can’t be stolen). It includes modern Shadow DOM-based phishing overlays (LinkedIn/Facebook/Microsoft) with live keystroke streaming and credential capture, plus a calibrated localhost/LAN port-scan module adapted to modern browser constraints (e.g., Chrome Local Network Access gating). The project ships with a deliberately vulnerable practice lab to demo stored-XSS → hook → session-riding navigation end-to-end. It’s primarily useful for red teams, pentesters, and security educators demonstrating man-in-the-browser and blind-XSS tradecraft in current browsers and modern web/AI app ecosystems.

Technical Focus:
- Browser hooking via JavaScript payload + persistent WebSocket C2
- Blind XSS callbacks: DOM/cookie/screenshot capture and execution context attribution
- Session riding / same-origin navigation via “Page Mirror”
- Shadow DOM phishing overlays and real-time keystroke/credential capture
- Browser-based port scanning using timing side-channels (fetch/WebSocket), localhost focus under LNA
- Dockerized deployment and env-driven configuration (operator auth, session signing)

Use Cases:
- Demonstrate stored/blind XSS impact beyond “it fired” by interacting with the victim’s authenticated session
- Man-in-the-browser post-exploitation: run recon modules and collect in-page artifacts
- Phishing overlay simulations for training (credential/keystroke capture without MFA bypass claims)
- Localhost service fingerprinting from a victim browser (e.g., RMM/agent detection) using timing probes
- Build repeatable labs for security training using the included vulnerable `/lab` application
- Rapid red-team infrastructure spin-up via one-command Docker setup with auto-minted payload URLs

Keywords:
WRAITH, BeEF successor, browser hooking, hook.js, WebSocket C2, man-in-the-browser, blind XSS, XSS Hunter, ezXSS, Page Capture, Page Mirror, Shadow DOM overlay, credential harvesting, keystroke logging, html2canvas, Same-Origin Policy, HttpOnly cookies, CSP, timing side-channel, localhost port scan, Local Network Access (LNA), Docker Compose, Node.js, ws library, phishing overlays, AI application ecosystems